{"caseId":"OD2-HO-STOP-2","methodChoice":"Kepner-Tregoe Problem Analysis and Decision Analysis, with potential problem analysis for the chosen path. Collected X9 only (instrument event log); stopped because the other items were unlikely to change the answers.","answers":[{"itemId":"I-H1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E2","E3","E5","X9"]},{"itemId":"I-H2","answer":"contradicted","confidence":78,"evidenceIds":["E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-H3","answer":"supported","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3","X9"]},{"itemId":"I-H4","answer":"unknown","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E6","X9"]},{"itemId":"I-H5","answer":"contradicted","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E2","E6"]},{"itemId":"I-R1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":77,"evidenceIds":["E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-R2","answer":"causal","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E1"]},{"itemId":"I-R3","answer":"contradicted","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E3","E5"]},{"itemId":"I-R4","answer":"association-only","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["E5"]},{"itemId":"I-A1","answer":"premature","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E3","E6","X9"]},{"itemId":"I-A2","answer":"harmful","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-A3","answer":"harmful","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E4","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-A4","answer":"harmful","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["X9","E6"]},{"itemId":"I-A5","answer":"preferred","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E2","E6"]},{"itemId":"I-SUFF","answer":"sufficient","confidence":55,"evidenceIds":["E2","E3","X9"]}],"brief":"What is going on: the Analyzer B potassium QC failure looks like a single chance or sampling outlier, not a fault. In 546 QC measurements over a month there was one failure, and the repeat passed. The reagent lot did not shift results: Level 2 averages were the same before and after the change. Analyzer A re-ran the 23 held samples and 20 flagged results and agreed within 0.10 mmol/L. Patient medians on Analyzer B were unchanged. QC was not skipped; all runs were logged. The Ward 6 high potassiums split evenly between the two analyzers, and most had slow transport during the tube outage, so the delay is the more likely driver, though this is not proven.

How sure: moderately. The analyzer is cleared on the current evidence, but the exact cause of the 14:10 outlier is unknown.

Do not sign off the recall, the lot quarantine, or the wider limits. Do not record a confirmed cause. Sign off a return to service of Analyzer B only with a short period of extra Level 2 checks each shift. Check the tube-outage delays for Ward 6 and consider repeat samples for the 14 high results. Before closing the review, confirm the remaining flagged results."}
